        .prose
          Fragment:md
            ## The Idea

            NMBL's structure-first syntax isn't only for HTML. The same
            indentation-based tags compile to an XML-ish string, and large
            markdown sections can stay as raw text — which makes NMBL a
            comfortable way to author **AI prompts** (Anthropic recommends XML
            tags to delimit prompt sections) or any structured document: light
            tags and attributes wrapped around prose.

            One source, two outputs:

            - render to an XML-like **string** to feed a model
            - parse to a structured **object** to read frontmatter and walk
              sections as data

            ## Authoring

            A standalone `.nmbl` file with YAML frontmatter, tags for structure,
            and `:md` blocks for prose. Author the prose with `:md` (not a bare
            `:` text block) so you keep markdown highlighting in your editor — the
            prompt target keeps it as text either way.

            ```nmbl title="prompts/summarize.nmbl"
            ---
            title: Summarize a document
            model: claude-opus-4-8
            tags: [summary, writing]
            ---
            system:md
              You are a precise summarization assistant.

            task
              instructions:md
                Summarize the document in **three bullet points**.
              document(source="report.pdf")
                body:md
                  Inject the document text here.
              output_format:md
                Return a markdown bulleted list.
            ```

            ## Render to a String

            Compile with the `prompt` target. Arbitrary tags pass through,
            attributes become metadata, and `:md` sections stay as text
            (re-indented to nest under their tag):

            ```ts
            import { compile } from '@nmbl-lang/core';

            const { html } = compile(source, { framework: 'prompt' });
            ```

            ```xml title="output"
            <system>
              You are a precise summarization assistant.
            </system>
            <task>
              <instructions>
                Summarize the document in **three bullet points**.
              </instructions>
              <document source="report.pdf">
                <body>
                  Inject the document text here.
                </body>
              </document>
              <output_format>
                Return a markdown bulleted list.
              </output_format>
            </task>
            ```

            The markdown stays literal — no escaping — which is what a model reads
            best. Runtime constructs like `@if` or `{@html}` are compile errors
            under this target: a prompt document is static structure, not a
            template.

            ## Parse to an Object

            The same file can be read as data through the `@nmbl-lang/core/structured`
            entry point:

            ```ts
            import { parseStructured } from '@nmbl-lang/core/structured';

            const { frontmatter, tree, body } = parseStructured(source);
            // frontmatter → { title, model, tags }
            // tree        → [{ tag: 'system', content: { mode: 'md', text } },
            //                { tag: 'task', children: [ … ] }]
            // body        → the template with frontmatter stripped (pass to compile())
            ```

            Content-mode sections come through as `{ mode, text }` with the
            markdown preserved as text; CSS shorthand (`#id` / `.class`) and
            explicit attributes fold into a flat `attrs` map.

            ## Loading Many Files

            `parseStructured` takes a plain string — it has no opinion about
            *where* the source comes from. A folder, a database row, an HTTP
            response all work the same. Reading a folder is just glue:

            ```ts
            import { readdirSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
            import { parseStructured } from '@nmbl-lang/core/structured';

            const prompts = Object.fromEntries(
              readdirSync('./prompts')
                .filter((f) => f.endsWith('.nmbl'))
                .map((f) => [f, parseStructured(readFileSync(`./prompts/${f}`, 'utf8'))]),
            );
            ```

            Now `prompts` is a registry you can filter by frontmatter, render on
            demand, or validate.

            ## When to Reach for This

            This shines when prompts have real nested structure or attributes and
            you manage many of them programmatically. For a single flat prompt,
            plain markdown with frontmatter is often enough — reach for NMBL here
            when the structure (and the one-source-two-outputs property) actually
            earns its keep.

